+ cat -n /Users/jakubigla/Project-Repositories/Personal/personal-site/.claude/worktrees/PS-3/docs/runbooks/hosting-first-apply.md
     1	# Runbook: first apply and DNS cut-over
     2	
     3	Every Terraform apply is attended (ADR-0010). Nothing here runs in CI and CI holds no AWS
     4	credential for these roots. You need: AWS credentials for the owner's account with rights to
     5	create S3, CloudFront, ACM, Route 53 records and Budgets; Terraform 1.10 or newer; the
     6	Route 53 hosted zone for `jacops.pl`, which already exists.
     7	
     8	The bucket names (`jacops-pl-terraform-state`, `jacops-pl-site`) are global; if S3 reports one
     9	taken, change it in `infra/bootstrap/main.tf` (and the `backend` block in
    10	`infra/environments/prod/main.tf`) or in the `site_hosting` module call, and commit that.
    11	
    12	## 1. Check the code locally
    13	
    14	```bash
    15	terraform fmt -check -recursive infra
    16	infra/policies/test.sh
    17	```
    18	
    19	## 2. Bootstrap the state bucket (once)
    20	
    21	```bash
    22	cd infra/bootstrap
    23	terraform init
    24	terraform apply
    25	```
    26	
    27	Its own state stays in `terraform.tfstate` in this directory. It is gitignored: keep it, never
    28	commit it. The bucket is versioned, encrypted, private and has `prevent_destroy`.
    29	
    30	## 3. First apply of the hosting root
    31	
    32	The current page is served by the old 2021 distribution, which holds the `jacops.pl` and
    33	`www.jacops.pl` aliases. CloudFront refuses to create a distribution with an alias another
    34	distribution already uses (`CNAMEAlreadyExists`), so the first apply runs with
    35	`serve_domain=false`: the distribution gets no aliases and the apex and www records are left
    36	alone. Everything else, the bucket policy included, is created.
    37	
    38	```bash
    39	cd infra/environments/prod
    40	terraform init
    41	terraform plan -var serve_domain=false -out=hosting.tfplan
    42	```
    43	
    44	Read the plan. Expect new: the site bucket, its public access block, ownership, encryption and
    45	policy, the CloudFront distribution with no aliases, origin access control, response headers
    46	policy, the router function, the certificate and its validation records, and the budget.
    47	Expect no apex or www record, and no WAF, NAT gateway, compute or logging resource. Then:
    48	
    49	```bash
    50	terraform apply hosting.tfplan
    51	```
    52	
    53	The apply creates the validation records, waits for the certificate and creates the
    54	distribution. The old page stays live: nothing it uses is touched.
    55	
    56	Confirm the budget subscription: AWS emails the contact address; open it if it asks you to
    57	confirm.
    58	
    59	## 4. Upload the site and check the new distribution
    60	
    61	Upload the built site to the bucket named by `terraform output site_bucket` (the deploy job
    62	does this once its role exists), then invalidate if needed. Check the distribution on its own
    63	`*.cloudfront.net` name (it has no alias yet, so a `jacops.pl` host header would reach the old
    64	distribution):
    65	
    66	```bash
    67	H=$(terraform output -raw distribution_domain)
    68	curl -sI https://"$H"/ | head -20             # 200 with the new site
    69	curl -sI https://"$H"/does-not-exist        # 404 with the custom page
    70	curl -sI http://"$H"/                         # 301 to https
    71	```
    72	
    73	You should see `strict-transport-security`, `content-security-policy`,
    74	`x-content-type-options`, `referrer-policy` and `x-frame-options` on every response. Do not go
    75	on until all three answer as shown.
    76	
    77	## 5. Cut-over: move the aliases, then the records
    78	
    79	The cut-over is the alias move. `aws cloudfront associate-alias` moves an alias from the old
    80	distribution to the new one in one call; the new one's certificate covers both names. From
    81	that moment CloudFront serves the alias from the new distribution, even to clients whose DNS
    82	still points at the old one, so the records can follow without downtime.
    83	
    84	1. Note the old state so the move can be reversed:
    85	
    86	   ```bash
    87	   ZONE=$(aws route53 list-hosted-zones-by-name --dns-name jacops.pl \
    88	     --query 'HostedZones[0].Id' --output text)
    89	   aws route53 list-resource-record-sets --hosted-zone-id "$ZONE" > old-records.json
    90	   OLD=<old distribution id>     # aws cloudfront list-distributions; the one with the aliases
    91	   OLD_H=$(aws cloudfront get-distribution --id "$OLD" --query 'Distribution.DomainName' --output text)
    92	   NEW=$(terraform output -raw distribution_id)
    93	   NEW_H=$(terraform output -raw distribution_domain)
    94	   ```
    95	
    96	   Keep `old-records.json` outside the repo.
    97	2. Lower the TTL of any non-alias apex or www record to 60 seconds and wait the old TTL.
    98	3. Prove you own the names to CloudFront: create `_jacops.pl` and `_www.jacops.pl` TXT records
    99	   whose value is `$NEW_H`:
   100	
   101	   ```bash
   102	   for n in _jacops.pl _www.jacops.pl; do
   103	     aws route53 change-resource-record-sets --hosted-zone-id "$ZONE" --change-batch \
   104	       '{"Changes":[{"Action":"UPSERT","ResourceRecordSet":{"Name":"'"$n"'","Type":"TXT","TTL":60,"ResourceRecords":[{"Value":"\"'"$NEW_H"'\""}]}}]}'
   105	   done
   106	   ```
   107	
   108	4. Move the aliases:
   109	
   110	   ```bash
   111	   aws cloudfront associate-alias --target-distribution-id "$NEW" --alias jacops.pl
   112	   aws cloudfront associate-alias --target-distribution-id "$NEW" --alias www.jacops.pl
   113	   ```
   114	
   115	5. Point the records at the new distribution with the full apply (`serve_domain` defaults to
   116	   true). The plan must show the four apex and www `A`/`AAAA` records created and **no change to
   117	   the distribution**: its aliases already match after the move. A plan that changes the
   118	   distribution means the move did not complete; stop and check `associate-alias`.
   119	
   120	   ```bash
   121	   terraform plan -out=hosting.tfplan
   122	   terraform apply hosting.tfplan
   123	   ```
   124	
   125	6. Verify `https://jacops.pl/` serves the new site, `https://www.jacops.pl/` redirects to the
   126	   apex with a 301, and `http://jacops.pl/` redirects to HTTPS.
   127	7. Rollback if anything is wrong: set the two TXT records' value to `$OLD_H`, run
   128	   `associate-alias --target-distribution-id "$OLD"` for both names (the old distribution's
   129	   certificate still covers them), then restore the apex and www records from
   130	   `old-records.json`, and investigate. Do not run `terraform apply` again until the cause is
   131	   fixed: with `serve_domain` true it re-points the records at the new distribution.
   132	8. Delete the two `_jacops.pl` and `_www.jacops.pl` TXT records once the site is verified.
   133	
   134	## 6. Retire the old site
   135	
   136	Keep the old resources for at least a week, until the new site has served traffic cleanly.
   137	The old 2021 Terraform state still holds the apex and www records, which now alias the new
   138	distribution through `allow_overwrite`: a `terraform destroy` there would delete them and take
   139	the site offline.
   140	
   141	1. In the old stack's root, remove its record resources from its state first, so no destroy
   142	   can touch them:
   143	
   144	   ```bash
   145	   terraform state list | grep aws_route53_record    # every record the old state holds
   146	   terraform state rm <each apex and www record address>
   147	   ```
   148	
   149	   Keep any record the old state holds that is not the apex or www site record (MX, TXT,
   150	   verification) out of the destroy the same way, or leave the old stack in place and delete the
   151	   old distribution and bucket by hand instead.
   152	2. Disable, then delete, the old distribution (it has no aliases left), and empty and delete the
   153	   old bucket.
   154	3. Remove every old record that served the old site, by name and type, as listed in
   155	   `old-records.json`: anything that pointed at the old distribution (`$OLD_H`) or at an S3
   156	   website or bucket endpoint, other than the four records step 5 replaced.
   157	4. Check that no record dangles: every CloudFront or S3 target left in the zone must be the new
   158	   distribution. This prints nothing when the zone is clean:
   159	
   160	   ```bash
   161	   aws route53 list-resource-record-sets --hosted-zone-id "$ZONE" --output json \
   162	     | jq -r --arg new "$NEW_H." '.ResourceRecordSets[]
   163	         | {n: .Name, t: .Type, v: ((.AliasTarget.DNSName // "") + " " + ([.ResourceRecords[]?.Value] | join(" ")))}
   164	         | select(.v | test("cloudfront\\.net|amazonaws\\.com"; "i"))
   165	         | select((.v | ascii_downcase | ltrimstr(" ") | rtrimstr(" ")) != ($new | ascii_downcase))
   166	         | "\(.n) \(.t) \(.v)"'
   167	   ```
   168	
   169	## 7. After the cut-over
   170	
   171	Raise the TTLs if you lowered them, and check the budget exists in AWS Budgets at 2 USD per
   172	month with alerts at 80% and 100% of actual spend. Later infrastructure changes follow steps
   173	1 and 3 only, without `-var serve_domain=false`: once the aliases have moved, the default
   174	keeps them and the records in place.
